r. If they discard one, they have one left, which might not help in forming a pung.\n\nConsidering all, maybe the best move is to discard a tile that is not part of any potential melds. For example, W7 is a single tile not part of any chow or pung. Discarding W7 might be the safest option.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The player's hand has potential for a concealed hand with chows and pairs, but needs to discard a tile that doesn't hinder future melds. Key tiles like W1, W2, W3 form a chow, and B3/F4 pairs are critical. Discarding W7, a single tile not part of any meld, is safest.\n\nReason: W7 is a mid-suit tile not part of any current or potential melds. Discarding it avoids giving opponents a tile for chows or pungs, while preserving key tiles for forming chows (W1-W3) and pairs (B3, F4). This maintains flexibility for future draws.\n\nAnswer: Play W7"}
