Or T4-5-6. No. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the B1 is part of a pair. But I only have one B1. \n\nHmm. Maybe I need to discard a tile to make this work. Let's see the valid actions. The valid actions are to play B1, W4, W2, W3, B3, T1, T2, T4. \n\nIf I play T4, maybe I can later form a chow with T1-2-3. But I don't have T3. \n\nIf I play W2, that would leave me with one W2, two W3, two W4. Maybe then I can form a chow of W2-3-4 and another of W3-4-5. But I don't have W5. \n\nIf I play W3, then I have W2, W2, W4, W4. Maybe two chows of W2-3-4 and W4-5-6. But no W3 or W5. \n\nIf I play B1, then the Dots are three B3s. So maybe a pung of B3 and a pair of B3? No, because I need four B3s for that. \n\nIf I play T1, then I have one T1 left. Maybe a pair of T2? No, I have one T2. \n\nHmm. This is tricky. Maybe the best move is to play a tile that doesn't block potential melds. For example, playing T4 might be a good idea because I don't have T3 or T5 to form a chow with T4. Similarly, playing W4 might be okay. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the best move is to play W4. Let's think. If I play W4, then I have one W4 left. The W2, W3, W4 could form a chow. But with two W2s and two W3s. If I have two chows of W2-3-4, that would require two W2s, two W3s, and two W4s. If I play one W4, I have one W4 left. So only one chow possible. Then I need to find other melds. \n\nAlternatively, maybe playing W4 would allow me to form a concealed hand with other melds. \n\nWait, another angle: The hand might not be a standard four melds and a pair. Maybe it's a special pattern. Let's see. For example, seven pairs. Do I have seven pairs? Let's see:\n\n- W2: 2 \u2192 one pair\n- W3: 2 \u2192 one pair\n- W4: 2 \u2192 one pair\n- T1: 2 \u2192 one pair\n- B3: 3 \u2192 one pair and one single\n- T2: 1, T4:1, B1:1 \u2192 singles\nSo total pairs: four pairs. Not seven. So no. \n\nWhat about all chows? If I have four chows and a pair. But how? Let's see. The W2-3-4 can be two chows. Then maybe a T1-2-3 chow (needs T3), but I don't have it. So no. \n\nHmm. Maybe the best move is to play T4, as it's a tile that's not part of any possible meld. Let me check the unshown tiles. The unshown tiles for T4 are 3. So there are still three T4s left. Playing T4 might not be too bad. \n\nAlternatively, playing W4. The unshown W4s are 4. So there are four left. \n\nBut I need to choose between the valid actions. The options are Play B1, W4, W2, W3, B3, T1, T2, T4. \n\nIf I play B1, then I have three B3s. Maybe form a pung of B3 and a pair of B3? No, but I can have a pung and a pair if I have four. Since I have three, maybe not. \n\nIf I play T1, then I have one T1 left. Maybe form a pair with another T1? No. \n\nIf I play W2, then I have one W2 left. The W3 and W4 could form a chow with another W2? No, because I need consecutive numbers. \n\nIf I play W3, similar issue. \n\nIf I play B3, then I have two B3s left. Can't form a pung. \n\nIf I play T2, then I have no T2 left. Maybe that's better. \n\nBut I'm not sure. Maybe the best move is to play T4, as it's not part of any potential meld. Let's go with that.\n", "output": "Evaluation: [The hand has potential for multiple chows in the Character suit and a pung in Dots. Playing T4 may not block future melds as it's not part of a viable chow.]\nReason: [Discarding T4 allows retaining key tiles for forming two chows of W2-3-4 and a pung of B3, while avoiding blocking potential melds.]\nAnswer: Play T4"}
